\subsection{Drop Only Shape Facts That a Store May Invalidate}

Losing a shape fact within a block can increase the number of
contexts compiled at its successors. Keep such facts only while
their dependencies are known. Consider a compiler that labels
a checked reference with a shape and gives each reference a
conservative set of abstract objects it may name. A property
store that may transition its receiver's shape kills facts whose
object sets intersect the receiver's set. This model has no
concurrent heap mutation, invokes no user code for the store,
and does not mutate shared shape descriptors. Treat an unknown
effect that can change any object as a kill of all shape facts;
immutable tag facts may survive. Assume the store is the last
operation before the outgoing edge in this walkthrough.

\begin{sudocode}[Transfer a context across a shape-changing store]
\State $out\gets$ copy of incoming context
\State $writes\gets$ abstract targets of the store
\ForAll{$(v,shape)$ in incoming shape facts}
  \If{$writes$ is unknown or $mayObjects[v]\cap writes\ne\emptyset$}
    \State remove shape fact for $v$ from $out$
      \Comment{Other names can denote the receiver}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State keep immutable tag facts \Comment{Tags are not heap fields}
\State project $out$ onto live successor parameters
\State \Return $out$
\end{sudocode}

Let $x$ and $y$ potentially name object $A$ checked at shape 2,
and $z$ name disjoint object $B$ at shape 7. A store through
$y$ changes $A$ from shape 2 to 3. On an execution where
$x=y$, the successor must be able to observe the new shape 3;
it cannot enter a version that assumes $x$ still has shape 2.
If field \texttt{n} changes from 9 to 12 along with that
transition, a generic read returns 12. The version can still
use the shape-7 layout to read a field of $z$, say 40. Test
both results to catch unsound retention and track the selected
context to catch needlessly dropping the disjoint fact.

The invariant is implication: actual values on the outgoing edge
must satisfy every remaining fact. Overapproximate alias sets
reduce precision but preserve that direction. If an interposed
setter can call arbitrary code, the local write set is no longer
sufficient even if the nominal receiver is disjoint.

\section{Heap facts, production scale and version budgets}

Chevalier-Boisvert and Feeley's \emph{Extending Basic Block Versioning with Typed Object Shapes} associates type information with property layouts so a checked shape can justify both the offset and type of a load. Shape facts are not immutable-value facts. An unknown call or a store through an alias can change the shape: discard the dependent fact, or use a proof or a renewed guard. In \emph{YJIT: A Basic Block Versioning JIT Compiler for CRuby}, Chevalier-Boisvert and colleagues describe an implementation that compiles lazily and can return to the interpreter by restoring its PC and stack pointer. This is not a contradiction of non-speculative edge reasoning: an implementation can combine proved context facts with speculative fast paths whose misses have separate exits.

Dossier Chapter~20 treats variants as persistent siblings, and Chapter~41 proposes choosing optimization granularity adaptively. BBV supplies an established block-sized design point, not the adaptive policy. In the blueprint's terms, the exploration plane could rank block contexts under a byte budget, but assurance still checks incoming facts and exit maps. Benchmark generic-only versus capped BBV while counting distinct contexts, stub hits, removed type tests and total code bytes. A program that cycles through many unrelated heap shapes tests whether the generic fallback bounds cost more effectively than compiling another plausible-looking specialization.
